rewrite_suggestion:
Ensure join-key type/collation compatibility and add supporting indexes (inspired by Cases 2 and 4). Verify that the surrogate-key columns being joined (d_date_sk/ss_sold_date_sk, ss_item_sk/i_item_sk, ss_customer_sk/c_customer_sk, c_current_addr_sk/ca_address_sk, ss_store_sk/s_store_sk) have identical data types — any implicit cast (e.g., int vs. varchar) disables index nested-loop joins, exactly the collation-mismatch problem in Case 2. Then create covering indexes aligned with the pre-filtered derived tables so each dimension lookup is an index-only scan and the fact-table range scan on the date dimension is driven by an index:

CREATE INDEX idx_date_ym    ON date_dim (d_year, d_moy, d_date_sk);
CREATE INDEX idx_item_cat   ON item (i_category, i_item_sk, i_brand_id, i_manufact_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_cust_bm    ON customer (c_birth_month, c_customer_sk, c_current_addr_sk);
CREATE INDEX idx_addr_state ON customer_address (ca_state, ca_address_sk, ca_zip);
CREATE INDEX idx_store_zip  ON store (s_store_sk, s_zip);
CREATE INDEX idx_ss_wc      ON store_sales (ss_wholesale_cost, ss_sold_date_sk, ss_item_sk, ss_customer_sk, ss_store_sk, ss_ext_sales_price);

With these, the rewritten query (Rewrite 2) executes as: index-range scan of the ~1 month of dates, index seek into item by 'Home', index seek into store_sales by wholesale-cost range per date, then point lookups on the remaining dimension indexes — with no table access needed for any column in the SELECT/WHERE lists.

Add composite/covering indexes supporting the join and filter keys (from Cases 1 and 4). Beyond rewriting the SQL, the queries above benefit from indexes such as:

```sql
-- fact table access path (date is the most selective filter)
CREATE INDEX ix_cr_date_cc ON catalog_returns (cr_returned_date_sk, cr_call_center_sk, cr_returning_customer_sk, cr_net_loss);
-- dimension access paths
CREATE INDEX ix_d_ym ON date_dim (d_year, d_moy, d_date_sk);
CREATE INDEX ix_cd_demo ON customer_demographics (cd_demo_sk, cd_marital_status, cd_education_status);
CREATE INDEX ix_hd_buy ON household_demographics (hd_buy_potential, hd_demo_sk);
CREATE INDEX ix_ca_gmt ON customer_address (ca_gmt_offset, ca_address_sk);
CREATE INDEX ix_c_keys ON customer (c_customer_sk, c_current_cdemo_sk, c_current_hdemo_sk, c_current_addr_sk);
```
The `catalog_returns` index includes `cr_net_loss` so the aggregation can be satisfied from the index alone (covering index), avoiding table lookups on the largest table; the `date_dim` index leads with the equality filter columns, mirroring Case 4's advice that the most selective filter column (duedate/due-date analog) should be the leading part of the key. Run `EXPLAIN` before and after to confirm the join order and index usage, as recommended in Cases 1, 3 and 4.
